  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-strict-and-weak-stationarity-for-time-series": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $X=(X_t)_{t\\in\\mathbb Z}$ be a real-valued stochastic process on one\nprobability space, so each $X_t$ is a real random variable and each finite\ntuple has its pushforward law\n([[def-stochastic-process-and-finite-dimensional-distributions]],\n[[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]],\n[[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]).\nWe call $X$ a **time series** here because its index set is $\\mathbb Z$.\n\nThe process is **strictly stationary** when for every integer $r\\ge1$, every\n$t_1,\\ldots,t_r\\in\\mathbb Z$, and every shift $m\\in\\mathbb Z$,\n$$\\mathcal L(X_{t_1+m},\\ldots,X_{t_r+m})=\\mathcal L(X_{t_1},\\ldots,X_{t_r}).$$\nThis condition concerns the full finite-dimensional laws and imposes no moment\ncondition by itself.\n\nThe process is **weakly stationary** when $E[|X_t|^2]<\\infty$ for every\n$t\\in\\mathbb Z$, its mean $\\mu:=E[X_t]$ is independent of $t$, and for every\n$h\\in\\mathbb Z$ the covariance\n$$\\gamma(h):=\\operatorname{Cov}(X_{t+h},X_t)$$\nis independent of $t$. Expectation, variance, and covariance have the meanings\nin [[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]] and\n[[def-moments-variance-and-covariance]]. Strict stationarity alone therefore\ndoes not supply the moment hypotheses needed for covariance or sample-mean\nclaims."
    ],
    "def-autocovariance-and-sample-autocovariance": [
      "## Definition\n\nFor a weakly stationary real process $X=(X_t)_{t\\in\\mathbb Z}$, its\n**autocovariance function** (ACVF) is\n$$\\gamma(h):=\\operatorname{Cov}(X_{t+h},X_t),\\qquad h\\in\\mathbb Z,$$\nwhich is independent of $t$ by [[def-strict-and-weak-stationarity-for-time-series]].\n\nFor a finite data vector $x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in\\mathbb R$, where $n\\ge1$, let\n$$\\bar x_n:=\\frac1n\\sum_{t=1}^n x_t.$$\nIts **biased sample ACVF** is the zero-extended sequence on $\\mathbb Z$ given by\n$$\\widehat\\gamma_n(h):=\\frac1n\\sum_{t=1}^{n-|h|}(x_{t+|h|}-\\bar x_n)(x_t-\\bar x_n),\\qquad |h|<n,$$\nand $\\widehat\\gamma_n(h):=0$ for $|h|\\ge n$. The absolute lag makes\n$\\widehat\\gamma_n(-h)=\\widehat\\gamma_n(h)$.\n\nFor comparison, define the **pair-count-normalized rescaling**\n$$\\widetilde\\gamma_n(h):=\\frac1{n-|h|}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-|h|}(x_{t+|h|}-\\bar x_n)(x_t-\\bar x_n),\\qquad |h|<n,$$\nand set it to zero for $|h|\\ge n$. Its denominator is positive on the stated\ndomain. The names specify the denominator convention; they assert no\nunbiasedness formula."
    ],
    "cor-ergodic-time-series-sample-mean-converges-a-s-and-l1": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Let $X=(X_t)_{t\\in\\mathbb Z}$ be a real strictly\nstationary process with $\\mathbb E|X_0|<\\infty$. Put\n$Y_k:=X_{k+1}$ for $k\\ge0$. If the canonical shift of the one-sided path law\nof $Y$ is ergodic, then\n$$\\bar X_n:=\\frac1n\\sum_{t=1}^nX_t\\longrightarrow\\mathbb E[X_0]$$\nalmost surely and in $L^1$. Here strict stationarity on $\\mathbb Z$ means\ninvariance of every finite-dimensional law under every common integer shift;\nthe one-sided ergodicity convention is that of\n[[def-stationary-process-and-canonical-shift]] and\n[[def-ergodic-measure-preserving-system]]."
    ],
    "thm-sample-acvf-properties-and-summable-covariance-mean-consistency": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $X=(X_t)_{t\\in\\mathbb Z}$ be weakly stationary, with mean $\\mu$ and\nautocovariance $\\gamma$, and define\n$\\bar X_n:=n^{-1}\\sum_{t=1}^nX_t$ for $n\\ge1$. Then\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X_n)=\\frac1{n^2}\\sum_{|h|<n}(n-|h|)\\gamma(h).$$\nIf $\\sum_{h\\in\\mathbb Z}|\\gamma(h)|<\\infty$, then\n$$0\\le\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X_n)\\le\\frac1n\\sum_{h\\in\\mathbb Z}|\\gamma(h)|,$$\nso $\\mathbb E[(\\bar X_n-\\mu)^2]\\to0$: the sample mean is consistent in $L^2$.\nThis covariance condition implies no mixing property and no central limit\ntheorem. For every finite real data vector, the zero-extended biased sample\nACVF $\\widehat\\gamma_n$ of\n[[def-autocovariance-and-sample-autocovariance]] has nonnegative Toeplitz\nquadratic forms."
    ],
